# Psaltery Second-Line

**Ingredients:** LITHUANIAN KANKLĖS + NEW ORLEANS SECOND-LINE / STREET-BRASS RHYTHM + PREPARED UPRIGHT PIANO + SNARE / BASS DRUM + BARITONE HORN + BASS CLARINET

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a spring residency in a retired fruit-canning and tin-label factory outside Kaunas, where kanklės player Rūta Vaitkutė was recording short plucked ostinatos in a loading hall while New Orleans drummer Ellis Baptiste rehearsed loose second-line figures with a stripped snare-and-bass-drum setup in the adjacent packing bay. The breakthrough came when Ellis stopped trying to force the kanklės into a conventional backbeat arrangement. Instead, he treated its alternating bass-and-treble string groups as a walking curb line: one plucked cell stayed stable while the drum accents drifted around it in push-pull parade time. Baritone horn, bass clarinet, and prepared upright piano were added to build a street-procession music where the plucked instrument acts neither as folk ornament nor harmonic pad, but as the fixed paving stones over which brass and drums swagger, lean, and temporarily lose balance.

Psaltery Second-Line is an acoustic-forward groove form built from the friction between rigid plucked repetition and elastic parade rhythm. Lithuanian kanklės supplies the fine-grained harmonic grid: open strings, paired-string resonance, short broken chords, repeated single-note hooks, pinched dyads, damped bass notes, and narrow modal turns. New Orleans second-line rhythm supplies the motion: loose-but-deep snare placement, bass-drum push, anticipatory accents, swung subdivisions, drag figures, brief stop-time breaks, and a walking sense of forward momentum that never becomes quantized funk.

The kanklės is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser above the treble field, one ribbon microphone near the lower body for wood and bass-string resonance, and one restrained room microphone aimed down the loading corridor. The close mic captures nail attack, sympathetic ringing, muted string contact, and small timing differences between hands. The ribbon gives the instrument enough body to coexist with brass without artificially boosting sub-bass. The room microphone remains low so the instrument keeps a clear pulse identity.

Players favor open fifths, modal thirds, suspended seconds, repeated tonic pedals, octave alternation, short descending three-note figures, diatonic clusters, damped bass plucks, and broken-chord patterns that can repeat for sixteen or thirty-two bars with only microscopic variation. Continuous arpeggiated “folk atmosphere” is avoided. The instrument must sound rhythmic before it sounds decorative.

The central compositional device is the **Curb Grid**. The kanklės establishes a repeating plucked pattern that acts as the physical street edge. Drums, brass, piano, and bass clarinet are allowed to lean ahead of it, fall behind it, or cross its accents, but the grid itself changes only at major structural points. The music creates groove by making the listener feel that several performers are walking the same route with different body weight.

A second device, the **Corner Turn**, changes orchestration without changing tempo. At the end of a four-, eight-, or sixteen-bar section, the ensemble executes a brief asymmetrical turnaround: snare drags forward, bass drum delays one expected impact, baritone horn holds through the implied downbeat, and the kanklės continues its pattern without resetting. The next phrase feels like the band has rounded a physical corner while the pavement underneath stayed continuous.

The rhythm section is acoustic and close. Snare should be crisp but not studio-perfect, with buzz rolls, drags, rimshots, ghost taps, and displaced backbeat accents. Bass drum is short and warm, sometimes landing slightly ahead of the snare’s expected weight. Cymbals are minimal: one small crash or splash can mark a procession turn, but long wash is avoided. Handclaps may appear in selected choruses, but no constant funk clap layer should flatten the swing.

Prepared upright piano provides short percussive harmony. Felt, paper, removable wedges, and light muting are used to shorten selected notes. The player favors clipped fourths, minor sevenths, sus2 dyads, octave stabs, low repeated single notes, and narrow three-note voicings. The piano often doubles neither the kanklės nor the brass; it occupies the gaps between them, acting like another piece of street furniture that catches passing accents.

Baritone horn is the principal brass voice. It plays warm low-mid punches, short upward sixths, held fourths, compact blues-inflected falls, breath-led swells, and occasional repeated-note shouts. It is intentionally used without a full horn section. The absence of trumpet and trombone stacks keeps the genre from becoming conventional brass-band pastiche. One baritone horn must carry enough personality to suggest procession without turning the arrangement into an imitation of a New Orleans ensemble.

Bass clarinet acts as the dark reed counterline. It plays chalumeau pedals, clipped syncopations, key-noise ghosts, minor-second slips, and short ascending figures that interlock with the baritone horn. It can temporarily replace the bass-drum low-mid weight during stop-time sections. Long jazz solos are excluded; the instrument functions as shadow brass made of wood and air.

Electric bass is optional and usually unnecessary. When present, it is muted, sparse, and restricted to roots, fifths, and walking approach notes. Most low-end motion should come from bass drum, baritone horn, left-hand piano, and low kanklės strings. This preserves the unusual acoustic balance.

Vocals are optional. When used, they are communal rather than lead-singer centered: short Lithuanian or English street calls, work-count responses, route numbers, crate labels, or one-line refrains shouted by two or three members. The vocal role is to momentarily widen the procession rather than create a pop chorus.

Effects are restrained. The kanklės remains mostly dry with a very short room. Piano may use a compact plate. Baritone horn gets a small warm chamber. Bass clarinet stays close and slightly dark. The drum bus can receive light tape saturation, but no aggressive sidechain, EDM compression, or synthetic widening is allowed. The genre should sound physically playable in one industrial hall without hidden electronic machinery.

The defining tension is geometric pluck versus bodily swing. The strings draw the curb. The drums and brass decide how recklessly to walk beside it.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 96–112 BPM, predominantly 4/4 with second-line-derived swing, displaced snare accents, drag figures, bass-drum anticipation, brief stop-time corners, and a steady kanklės ostinato that acts as the reference grid.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian and Mixolydian centers, open fifths, modal thirds, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, diatonic clusters, occasional blue-note inflection in brass, and long one- or two-chord zones where rhythm and voicing carry the form.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Curb Grid** — a repeating kanklės pattern remains structurally stable while drums and brass lean ahead of or behind its accents.
2. **Corner Turn** — brief ensemble turnarounds change perceived direction without changing tempo or restarting the plucked grid.
3. **Single-Horn Rule** — baritone horn acts as the main brass voice; full trumpet/trombone section stacking is avoided.
4. **Wood-Air Counterline** — bass clarinet answers or shadows baritone horn with darker reed articulations rather than functioning as a soloist.
5. **Prepared-Piano Gapwork** — upright-piano stabs occupy spaces between kanklės and drums instead of doubling either part continuously.
6. **No-Quantized Parade Rule** — second-line feel must come from human push-pull timing; hard grid correction and programmed swing templates cannot define the groove.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through denser snare drags, stronger bass-drum anticipation, wider baritone-horn intervals, more active bass-clarinet counterlines, brighter kanklės attack, and thicker communal shouts. Climaxes remain mobile and street-sized rather than cinematic.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Psaltery Second-Line formed inside a decommissioned fruit-canning and tin-label factory on the industrial edge of Kaunas. The building retained green loading doors, dented sheet-metal tables, faded orchard logos, pallet scales, roller conveyors, burlap fruit sacks, hand-painted crate numbers, chipped tile walls, and a long loading apron that ran parallel to a shallow rail spur.

Kanklės player **Rūta Vaitkutė** had been using the hall to document different damping methods on a modern concert kanklės and a smaller traditional instrument. She became interested in how repeated bass-and-treble alternation created the sensation of walking even when the harmony barely changed.

New Orleans drummer **Ellis Baptiste** was in Lithuania for a separate percussion residency and was practicing parade snare on the loading apron. His first attempt to join Rūta followed her accents too closely and sounded stiff. On the next pass he let the snare drag across her pattern, pushed one bass-drum note early, delayed another, and refused to land squarely on every plucked downbeat. The kanklės suddenly felt less like a recital instrument and more like fixed pavement under moving feet.

Prepared-piano player **Milda Petrauskaitė** joined after finding an upright piano in the old staff canteen and deadening several notes with removable felt strips. Baritone-horn player **Jonas Reed** added a single low brass voice instead of building a full horn section. Bass-clarinetist **Sofija Clarke** completed the ensemble with dark reed counterlines that filled the spaces a conventional sousaphone or bass guitar might have occupied.

Their first performances were called **loading marches**. The musicians moved slowly from one numbered loading bay to another while the audience stood along the conveyor lanes. The kanklės remained on a wheeled packing table, so the “procession” was created by rhythm and room perspective rather than literal marching.

The visual language developed around oxidized orchard green, faded tin red, cream paper labels, raw birch wood, dull brass, black wool, concrete grey, and warm incandescent warehouse lamps. The unofficial rule is that the groove must feel as if it can turn a corner without losing the plucked pattern.

