## 2. Tin Labels Swing After the Roller Stops

**Story & Context:** At 110 BPM, the track begins with the conveyor-room piano rather than drums. Milda plays a deadened two-note sus2 figure while Rūta answers with low-high kanklės alternation. Ellis enters with the EP’s most pronounced second-line feel: loose snare ghosts, one bass-drum anticipation before beat three, and a short drag into beat four. Jonas uses short baritone-horn falls rather than held notes. Sofija plays clipped chalumeau syncopations that sometimes occupy the exact space where a conventional bass guitar note would sit.

Halfway through, the full drum kit stops for four bars but the groove remains readable through kanklės, baritone horn, bass clarinet, and muted piano attacks. When Ellis returns, he enters with a snare pickup one eighth-note earlier than expected, making the resumed pulse feel wider rather than merely louder. The track ends with one dry tin-sheet strike recorded from the room, used once as a final punctuation rather than a recurring industrial sample.

