## 3. Orchard Gate Turns Without Changing Tempo

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 98 BPM and uses the widest spaces on the EP. Rūta plays a slower kanklės figure built from tonic, fifth, minor seventh, and a muted octave. Ellis limits himself to bass drum, rim clicks, and occasional snare drags. Milda plays isolated low piano notes. Jonas holds long baritone-horn fourths that overlap Rūta’s repeated pattern, while Sofija uses soft bass-clarinet key noise and short low pedals.

Each eight-bar section contains one Corner Turn placed in a different part of the measure. The first turn occurs at the expected barline; the second begins on beat three; the third starts just after beat one. The tempo never changes and the kanklės pattern never resets, but the ensemble repeatedly gives the sensation of taking a different physical route. In the final passage, drums disappear completely. Rūta continues the ostinato while Jonas and Sofija sustain a low open fifth around it. The last sound is one damped kanklės bass note followed by the natural room decay of the loading hall.

